 know it would be  better if the president read the  materials and studied them more  carefully I think that that written  materials tend to be a more efficient  way to convey information certainly the  president's always free to ask questions  and that's to be encouraged but it's  it's despite what some advisors to the  president have said he does not consume  as much intelligence as he should let's  talk if we may about the 2018 murder of  the Saudi journalist Jamarcus Auggie by  Saudi operatives in Istanbul October the  15th that year Trump decided to stand by  the Saudi leadership and even wrote  their excuse for them suggesting it was  a rogue operation but it wasn't a rogue  operation was it well you know I'm not  going to get into the to the the  intelligence that the United States had  on that I can say the president decided  that he was going to stand by the Saudis  and because of the importance of the  relationship if that position I think  was was in a way verified by Vladimir  Putin in a conversation I had with him  in Moscow who said look you don't want  to sell weapons to the Saudis you don't  have to I'd be happy to sell it to them  is that what it really comes down to  who's going to be able to sell weapons  to whom you gave a free pass to a  government that brazenly chops up its  political opponents in other people's  countries and not just to the Saudis but  to every other  state the US does big business with the  tortures and murders its own people  doesn't that worry you well you know as  Winston Churchill said if if the devil  were to have opposed Adolf Hitler he  would have found that in his heart for a  kind word to Satan on the floor of the  House of Commons you don't you don't  always get to pick who your allies are  in a difficult unstable part of the  world nobody in the administration  condoned what happened to Khashoggi  there's no question about it but we live  in a harsh world you live in a world  where i wonder how many of the untold  masses jailed for having opinions in  Saudi Arabia and other despotic states  that the u.s. befriends would go along  with your statement that the greatest  hope for freedom of mankind is the u.s.  if a country that can finesse mr.  Khashoggi murder is the best hope for  mankind's freedom then god help mankind  No  well look you're entitled to your  opinions I'm glad and proud to be an  American citizen  there isn't any country in the world  that has done more to protect human  freedom than the United States period  close quote let's talk about Iran if we  may so many bad deals to kill you once  wrote so little time the one that you  really had in your sights over many  years was the Iran nuclear deal wasn't  it unenforceable men you better want  unenforceable you called it I'm  verifiable and inadequate in duration  and scope but you wanted to kill that  deal with nothing to put in its place  over the objections of key members of  Israel's military and intelligence staff  who regard 'iron as their number-one  adversary many of them didn't see it  your way did they well I think you have  it wrong about Israel I think support  for the u.s. getting out of the Iran  nuclear deal in Israel is overwhelming  you may have noticed some things are  happening in Iran they're centrifuge  assembly plant at Natanz apparently had  a severe accident that that they've not  revealed fully there are other  oceans going on around the country I  don't know who's doing it but I'm glad  somebody's doing it I don't know whether  it's the precursor to taking down the  entire nuclear weapons and ballistic  missile program in Iran but it certainly  is a step in that direction the fact is  I don't trust the ayatollahs and the  regime I think they consistently  withheld information from international  inspectors they lied about the nature of  the program for 20-plus years until they  were caught time and time again in those  lies I don't think they ever made a  strategic decision to give up nuclear  weapons and I think the u.s. decision to  pull out of the deal  caught them by surprise and left them in  a very difficult position it's a hugely  unpopular regime in Iran and hopefully  the day will come when the ayatollahs  are swept into the dustbin of history if  we can just go back to Ukraine you  decided not to give testimony at the  house impeachment hearing you didn't  like the way the Democrats handled the  process you called it impeachment  malpractice if you thought the president  was abusing his power wasn't it more  important to get that out than worrying  about procedural issues isn't it more  important to get a result again you're  back in the virtues signaling posturing  mode of politics and and that's just not  the way I do things the Democratic  malpractice which is what it was left  the situation worse than it was before  the impeachment why because the way they  conducted the impeachment in a very  partisan fashion essentially guaranteed  a partisan outcome and the outcome was  that the Senate acquitted Trump so the  impeachment advocates said that by the  house action of impeaching the president  he will be deterred from engaging in the  kind of conduct we saw in Ukraine but  they had no way of getting 2/3 of the  Senate to vote to convict  so having acquitted Trump they they  don't only did not deter him they  empowered him they  moved the impeachment guardrail is a  huge mistake by publishing this book  what I've done is to try and lay the  facts out before the real judges not  Congress but the American people we have  an election in four months the American  people will speak I trust their judgment  more than Congress but the election  isn't a substitute for the  constitutional provisions for removing a  president from office what do you think  elections are if they're not the  principle constitutional path to doing  just that  impeachment has occurred four times in  American history and no president has  ever been convicted only Nixon resigned  and what distinguishes the Watergate  impeachment proceeding from the three  that failed Andrew Johnson Bill Clinton  and Donald Trump  the Democrats worked in Watergate with  Republican Sam Ervin of North Korea  North Carolina worked with Howard Baker  of Tennessee and they produced a growing  feeling on a bipartisan basis that Nixon  had to go by ignoring that relatively  recent precedent the Democrats  guaranteed the outcome we got the and  this this is a I think a very  fundamental point about democratic  theory the principled way you remove  presidents is by elections not by  impeachment we're four months away from  the next of those elections  what if Trump loses by a small margin do  you expect him to go quietly or organize  some kind of bizarre national emergency  to try and stay where he is well maybe  you would tell me what evidence you have  that he's planning to create a bizarre  national emergency you know Arizona didn  t that the the votes will be the most  corrupt election in the history of our  country and we cannot let this happen so  you're making the same mistake as many  Trump critics you're exaggerating  without adequate evidence and I know  your question I know from your questions  you obviously despise Trump that that's  fine I understand that but but the way  you're approaching it makes it difficult  for those of us who are opposed to him  getting another term to make the case to  Americans who want to know what the  facts are that that's something but you  know the the people will decide here in  four months and we'll see what the  outcome is before you get hyperthyroid  about it let's see what happens John  Bolton thanks very much for being on  conflict zone I enjoyed it  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]
 

